  translation: "1. Arjuna said: If you hold knowledge to be better than action, O Janardana, why then do you set me to this terrible action, O Keshava?\n2. With a speech that seems confused, you confuse my understanding, as it were. Decide, and tell me the one path by which I may reach the good.\n3. The blessed Lord said: In this world a twofold devotion was taught by me of old, O sinless one: by the yoga of knowledge for the Sankhyas, and by the yoga of action for the yogins.\n4. A man does not reach freedom from action by not beginning actions, nor does he reach that perfection by renunciation alone.\n5. For no one ever stays even a moment without doing some action, since everyone is driven to act, helpless, by the qualities born of nature.\n6. He who holds back the organs of action and sits remembering the objects of the senses with his mind, that man, his mind bewildered, is called a man of false conduct.\n7. But he who controls the senses with the mind and, unattached, undertakes the yoga of action with the organs of action, O Arjuna: he excels.\n8. Do the action that is laid down for you. Action is better than not acting, and without action even the keeping of your body would not come to pass.\n9. Other than action done for sacrifice, this world is bound by action. Do action for that end, O son of Kunti, free of attachment.\n10. Of old, having brought forth creatures together with sacrifice, the lord of creatures said: By this, bring forth increase. Let this be your yielder of the objects you wish.\n11. With this, foster the gods, and may those gods foster you. Fostering one another, you will reach the highest good.\n12. The gods, fostered by sacrifice, will give you the enjoyments you wish for. He who enjoys what they have given, without giving to them, is a thief.\n13. The good, who eat what is left from sacrifice, are released from every stain. But the wicked, who cook for their own sake, eat only evil.\n14. From food, beings come to be, and from rain comes the arising of food. From sacrifice comes rain, and sacrifice arises from action.\n15. Know action as arising from the Veda, and the Veda as arising from the Imperishable. Therefore the Veda, which reaches everything, is forever set firm in sacrifice.\n16. He who here does not keep turning the wheel set going in this way lives in vain, O son of Pritha: a man whose life is sin, taking his pleasure in the senses.\n17. But the man who delights in the self alone, who is content with the self and satisfied in the self alone: for him nothing remains to be done.\n18. He has no end to gain by what he does, and no harm here from what he does not do. Nor has he any end that depends on any being.\n19. Therefore, unattached, always perform the action that is to be done. A man who performs action without attachment reaches the highest.\n20. By action alone, indeed, Janaka and the others reached perfection. You too should act, looking to the holding of the world to its course.\n21. Whatever a leading man does, that very thing other people do. Whatever standard he sets, the world follows it.\n22. There is nothing I must do in the three worlds, O son of Pritha, and nothing left unobtained that I still have to obtain. Even so, I engage in action.\n23. If I did not tirelessly engage in action, O son of Pritha, men would follow my path in every way.\n24. If I did not perform action, these worlds would perish. I would be the maker of the confusion of orders, and I would harm these creatures.\n25. As the unknowing, attached to the action, perform it, O Bharata, so should the one who knows perform it, unattached, wishing to hold the world together.\n26. He should not shake the conviction of the unknowing who cling to action. The one who knows, himself engaged, should have them perform every action while he does the same.\n27. Actions are done on every side by the qualities of nature. One whose mind is deluded by the thought \"I\" imagines, \"I am the doer.\"\n28. But he who knows the truth of how the qualities and actions are distinguished, O mighty-armed, understands that the qualities move among the qualities, and he is not attached.\n29. Those deluded by the qualities of nature cling to the actions of the qualities. He who knows the whole should not shake those dull people, who do not know it.\n30. Laying every action on me with a mind turned to the self, and becoming free of hope and of the thought \"mine,\" fight, free of the fever of grief.\n31. Men who always practice this teaching of mine, with faith and without finding fault, are released from actions.\n32. But those who find fault with this and do not practice my teaching, know them to be lost, deluded about every kind of knowledge, and without judgment.\n33. Even one who knows acts in keeping with his own nature. Beings follow their nature. What will restraint do?\n34. Attraction and aversion stand in the object of every sense. One should not come under their power. They are the ones who block his road.\n35. One's own duty, even without its good qualities, is better than another's duty well performed. Death in one's own duty is better. Another's duty brings danger.\n36. Arjuna said: Driven by what does a man do evil even without wishing to, O descendant of Vrishni, as if compelled by force?\n37. The blessed Lord said: This is desire, and this is anger, born of the quality called rajas. Know it here as the enemy, of great appetite and a great evil.\n38. As fire is covered by smoke, and a mirror by dust, as an embryo is covered by the caul, so is this covered by that.\n39. Knowledge is covered by this, the constant enemy of the one who knows, O son of Kunti: by desire, hard to fill, a fire that is never enough.\n40. The senses, the mind, and the understanding are called its seat. Covering knowledge by means of these, it bewilders the embodied one.\n41. Therefore, O best of the Bharatas, first restrain the senses, and cast off this evil, the destroyer of knowledge and of knowing.\n42. They say the senses are higher, and the mind is higher than the senses. Higher than the mind is the understanding, and higher than the understanding is he.\n43. Thus knowing what is higher than the understanding, and steadying the self by the self, strike down that enemy, O mighty-armed: desire, hard to reach.",
